Run child-command redirection regression
=======================================

Build with test-dos-runcommandredir or test-dos-runcommandredir-quick.
Both are selected beneath the DOS component's normal and quick test groups.
Run runcommandredir beside runcommandchild, or pass the child's path as its
first argument. Child paths are quoted in every launch case.

The parent creates an owned directory in RAM:. The child reads its Input(),
writes those exact bytes to Output(), and writes a separate completion file.
This checks that the child really runs, independently of SystemTagList's return
code. Before the fix, RunBack's repeated NIL form returns success without
executing the child; the test fails on the missing completion file.

Coverage includes RunBack's exact NIL form, distinct outer and child input/output
files, case-insensitive Run with QUIET, a plain Run with child redirections,
quoted child paths, failed outer input, ordinary duplicate redirections, and
subsequent shell usability. Successful runs remove their temporary files;
failed asynchronous launches retain evidence rather than reuse their paths.

Expected result: RUN COMMAND REDIR TEST: 0 failures.

Quoted command regression
-------------------------
Run runquotedcommand beside runcommandchild. It copies the child into its own
RAM: directory under names containing spaces, stars and double quotes, then
checks the child's completion file and redirected output. Its argument and
redirection paths also contain spaces. Coverage includes QUIET, mixed case,
the COMMAND keyword (with and without '='), and outer Run redirections.
An additional check keeps commands entered after Run's '?' help prompt working.
The unchanged Run loses the child filename's quoting and fails the first case.
Expected result: RUN QUOTED TEST: 0 failures.

For a focused external probe, runcommandredir still accepts a child's path
followed by PLAIN to exercise only the ordinary Run control.

